1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:09,050
He did not die.

2
00:00:09,449 --> 00:00:15,120
The narrator will deal with that immediately, because
two hundred years of popular accounts

3
00:00:15,140 --> 00:00:18,059
have made a mystery of it and
there is not one.

4
00:00:18,460 --> 00:00:24,256
What the System took back, when it went, was the
catalogue and the panels and the Points

5
00:00:24,276 --> 00:00:29,874
and the ledger — and about eleven per cent of what
Albert Wesker's body could do. His

6
00:00:29,894 --> 00:00:35,558
strength fell by roughly that. His reflexes. The
regeneration slowed to something that

7
00:00:35,578 --> 00:00:41,309
a very good hospital could match. He aged, from that
morning, at approximately one year

8
00:00:41,329 --> 00:00:41,969
in ninety.

9
00:00:42,369 --> 00:00:48,261
He was two hundred and sixty years old and had
become, in the space of about a minute,

10
00:00:48,281 --> 00:00:54,860
an extremely healthy man of indeterminate middle age with
no supernatural properties whatsoever,

11
00:00:54,880 --> 00:01:00,702
who owned nothing that anybody could take from him
and had no access to anything that

12
00:01:00,722 --> 00:01:01,939
anybody could not.

13
00:01:02,340 --> 00:01:08,079
What it left was everything it had ever given him: a
building on a river, a valley with

14
00:01:08,099 --> 00:01:13,970
a city in it, a house in a forest, four hundred
thousand people who did not age, nineteen

15
00:01:13,990 --> 00:01:19,795
billion who did not die of anything ordinary, a drive
that moved ships without expelling

16
00:01:19,815 --> 00:01:25,422
mass, and a species on nine hundred worlds that had
spent a hundred and seventy years

17
00:01:25,442 --> 00:01:27,210
learning to argue with him.

18
00:01:27,609 --> 00:01:33,091
*What is given is given.* He read that clause four
times in two hundred and sixty years

19
00:01:33,111 --> 00:01:38,340
and never once asked what it meant, and it turned
out to mean exactly what it said.

20
00:01:38,739 --> 00:01:40,810
He stayed in Wilmington.

21
00:01:41,209 --> 00:01:48,671
The narrator finds this the single strangest fact in
the account and has no explanation

22
00:01:48,691 --> 00:01:56,238
for it beyond the one Wesker gave, in 2289, to a
historian named Adaeze Kalu the Fourth,

23
00:01:56,258 --> 00:02:03,720
a descendant of the woman who had asked him in 2069
who decides what the alternative is

24
00:02:03,740 --> 00:02:04,579
next time.

25
00:02:05,099 --> 00:02:12,932
I am told I should go to Ochoa, or to the ice, or to
one of the nine hundred. Everybody

26
00:02:12,952 --> 00:02:15,369
who suggests it means well.

27
00:02:15,650 --> 00:02:22,333
I came into this world at five fifty-eight in the
morning on the fourteenth of September

28
00:02:22,353 --> 00:02:29,494
2026 in a chair on this floor, and I have been in a
hurry ever since, and I have now finished.

29
00:02:29,514 --> 00:02:36,350
This is where the thing started. It seems reasonable
to be here for the part where nothing

30
00:02:36,370 --> 00:02:36,959
happens.

31
00:02:37,360 --> 00:02:43,661
He resigned from the Directorate in 2287. The
Assembly accepted the resignation after

32
00:02:43,681 --> 00:02:50,056
a debate of nine hours in which four delegates
argued that he should be prevented from

33
00:02:50,076 --> 00:02:56,750
resigning, which the narrator considers the funniest
thing in two hundred and sixty years.

34
00:02:57,150 --> 00:03:03,224
The chair at the long stone table stayed empty.
There were then three empty chairs at

35
00:03:03,244 --> 00:03:09,390
that table, and nobody was ever told why any of them
were, and by 2400 there were nine

36
00:03:09,410 --> 00:03:14,840
and the practice had become a tradition with
four incompatible explanations.

37
00:03:15,240 --> 00:03:21,333
He did not take another position. He voted in Raccoon
City's municipal elections by post.

38
00:03:21,353 --> 00:03:27,171
He answered correspondence, of which there was a
great deal, personally, in longhand,

39
00:03:27,191 --> 00:03:33,284
at a rate of about eighteen letters a week, and the
archive of those letters runs to four

40
00:03:33,304 --> 00:03:37,748
hundred volumes and is the other primary
source for this account.

41
00:03:38,149 --> 00:03:44,400
Evelyn Marsh came to see him about four times a year.
She was one hundred and eighty-eight

42
00:03:44,420 --> 00:03:50,393
and did not age and never would; he was two hundred
and sixty-four and had begun, very

43
00:03:50,413 --> 00:03:51,299
slightly, to.

44
00:03:51,818 --> 00:03:53,188
You've got grey at the temple.

45
00:03:53,588 --> 00:03:59,088
I have been aware of it for twenty-four months and I
have been waiting for somebody to

46
00:03:59,108 --> 00:04:00,050
have the nerve.

47
00:04:00,450 --> 00:04:01,480
Does it frighten you?

48
00:04:02,320 --> 00:04:08,450
(very soft) It is the most interesting thing that
has happened to me since 2062.

49
00:04:08,850 --> 00:04:14,680
They sat on a bench by a river in Delaware, the two
oldest things on the planet, and the

50
00:04:14,700 --> 00:04:20,662
narrator records that they talked for four hours about
a tram extension, a granddaughter's

51
00:04:20,682 --> 00:04:26,511
marriage, and whether the Assembly's fisheries
directive was correctly drafted, and that

52
00:04:26,531 --> 00:04:29,302
neither of them mentioned the System once.

53
00:04:29,704 --> 00:04:35,225
The narrator will give you the funeral, briefly,
because four people came and it is the

54
00:04:35,245 --> 00:04:38,473
only funeral in this account with
no cameras at it.

55
00:04:38,874 --> 00:04:44,494
He had left four lines of instruction and the fourth
was that nobody was to be invited.

56
00:04:44,514 --> 00:04:50,004
The Directorate honoured it, to the letter, and
then discovered that it had no way of

57
00:04:50,024 --> 00:04:52,014
preventing anybody from coming.

58
00:04:52,413 --> 00:04:58,997
Eleven people came. Evelyn Marsh. Her daughter Mira
Reiss-Marsh and two of her grandchildren,

59
00:04:59,017 --> 00:05:05,175
who had never met him. The Red Queen, through a
relay, who had never attended anything.

60
00:05:05,195 --> 00:05:11,352
A gardener from the estate at Arklay, aged a hundred
and forty, whose great-grandmother

61
00:05:11,372 --> 00:05:14,264
had been summoned into existence in 2027.

62
00:05:14,663 --> 00:05:20,002
And four people from the valley who had read the
notice in the municipal register, and

63
00:05:20,022 --> 00:05:25,797
who had grandparents in that cemetery, and who were not
quite able to explain to a journalist

64
00:05:25,817 --> 00:05:27,603
afterwards why they had come.

65
00:05:28,124 --> 00:05:32,414
He's being buried in the made section. That's ours.
Nobody asked us about that either,

66
00:05:32,434 --> 00:05:36,574
and I'm here to see it done, and I don't know if
that's respect or the other thing.

67
00:05:37,293 --> 00:05:41,425
(very slow) My grandmother had a name for the town
she grew up in and couldn't say it.

68
00:05:41,445 --> 00:05:44,563
She used to laugh about it. She laughed
about it for sixty years.

69
00:05:44,843 --> 00:05:48,917
So I came up the hill to watch him be put in the
ground next to her, and I'll tell you

70
00:05:48,937 --> 00:05:53,154
what I thought when they did it, which is nothing at
all, and I've been trying for a week

71
00:05:53,174 --> 00:05:56,487
to work out whether that's the worst thing
I've ever felt or the best.

72
00:05:56,887 --> 00:06:02,940
There is a stone. It is the same size as the eleven
thousand around it. It carries a name,

73
00:06:02,960 --> 00:06:09,080
two dates, and nothing else, and the second date is
wrong by two days because the municipal

74
00:06:09,100 --> 00:06:12,657
clerk made an error and nobody has
ever corrected it.

75
00:06:13,057 --> 00:06:19,154
This account ends in a schoolroom, four hundred and
eleven light years from where it began,

76
00:06:19,174 --> 00:06:24,868
on the two hundredth anniversary of the day the
*Christina* left Dock One with eleven

77
00:06:24,888 --> 00:06:29,036
hundred people aboard and a structures
engineer on the bridge.

78
00:06:29,435 --> 00:06:35,018
There are nineteen children in it, aged about nine.
Four of them are Progenitor lineage

79
00:06:35,038 --> 00:06:40,491
and will still be nine hundred years old when the
rest are four generations gone, and

80
00:06:40,511 --> 00:06:45,965
they all know this about each other and have worked
out their own etiquette about it,

81
00:06:45,985 --> 00:06:50,666
which adults have been studying for a century
and have never improved on.

82
00:06:51,065 --> 00:06:58,277
The lesson is the founding of the colony. There is a
portrait on the wall of Mira Halder,

83
00:06:58,297 --> 00:07:05,427
who built it and then resigned, and a smaller one of
Theresa Okonkwo-Vance, who told the

84
00:07:05,447 --> 00:07:09,895
Veth Hegemony no in 2141 and
broadcast it in the clear.

85
00:07:10,295 --> 00:07:16,309
There is no portrait of Albert Wesker. There are
four in the building — in the history

86
00:07:16,329 --> 00:07:22,482
department, in a cabinet, with the other founding
material — and the narrator notes that

87
00:07:22,502 --> 00:07:28,655
this is the arrangement he requested in 2247 in a
letter to the colony's first education

88
00:07:28,675 --> 00:07:33,286
board, and that they honoured it, and that
he wrote to thank them.

89
00:07:33,805 --> 00:07:38,698
— and that's the bit everyone remembers. But
somebody tell me the important part. Why

90
00:07:38,718 --> 00:07:39,795
is it the building?

91
00:07:40,196 --> 00:07:41,876
Because it just appeared.

92
00:07:42,276 --> 00:07:43,866
It did. Anyone else?

93
00:07:44,266 --> 00:07:46,046
Because nobody noticed.

94
00:07:46,886 --> 00:07:51,995
Yes. That's the one. Eight billion people, and the
buildings and the cities and the medicine

95
00:07:52,015 --> 00:07:57,069
and the ships all arrived, and nobody noticed, because
they came with the paperwork already

96
00:07:57,089 --> 00:07:57,348
done.

97
00:07:57,627 --> 00:08:02,504
And it took this species two hundred years to work
out that the dangerous part was never

98
00:08:02,524 --> 00:08:05,897
the man. It was that we are extremely
easy to hand things to.

99
00:08:06,298 --> 00:08:11,968
The narrator will stop editorialising now and
simply set down the last four facts.

100
00:08:12,367 --> 00:08:20,330
Albert Wesker died on the eleventh of June 2294, at
Wilmington, aged two hundred and sixty-eight,

101
00:08:20,350 --> 00:08:27,326
of a cerebral haemorrhage, which is an ordinary
thing that happens to ordinary people

102
00:08:27,346 --> 00:08:35,062
and which no hospital on earth was able to prevent
because by then he was, in every measurable

103
00:08:35,082 --> 00:08:37,366
respect, an ordinary person.

104
00:08:37,767 --> 00:08:43,639
He left instructions of four lines. No state
funeral, no monument, no name on anything

105
00:08:43,659 --> 00:08:49,669
that is not already carrying it. He was buried in the
cemetery on the hill above Raccoon

106
00:08:49,689 --> 00:08:55,493
City, in the section for the people who were made
there, among eleven thousand graves

107
00:08:55,513 --> 00:09:01,385
that had been dug for people who never existed until
a Saturday in 2028, and his stone

108
00:09:01,405 --> 00:09:03,235
is the same size as theirs.

109
00:09:03,636 --> 00:09:10,462
The Umbrella Directorate flew its flag at half mast
for the third and last time, and then,

110
00:09:10,482 --> 00:09:17,308
in 2301, dissolved itself by a vote of its own board,
and its assets went to the Assembly,

111
00:09:17,328 --> 00:09:24,154
and the argument about what to do with them ran for
forty years and was, by every account,

112
00:09:24,174 --> 00:09:25,067
magnificent.

113
00:09:25,466 --> 00:09:27,047
And the last fact is this.

114
00:09:27,447 --> 00:09:33,069
In four hundred and eleven worlds, an entity that
has never been located and never been

115
00:09:33,089 --> 00:09:38,582
described gave four hundred and eleven people a
catalogue with prices in it, and paid

116
00:09:38,602 --> 00:09:44,225
them in the love of the populations they made, and
four hundred and one of those worlds

117
00:09:44,245 --> 00:09:45,976
became perfect and stopped.

118
00:09:46,376 --> 00:09:52,501
Seven are beautiful and silent. One is a library
that ends when its librarian does.

119
00:09:52,902 --> 00:09:58,571
And one is a loud, argumentative, badly governed
sphere of nineteen billion people spread

120
00:09:58,591 --> 00:10:04,260
across nine hundred worlds, who found out exactly
what had been done to them and to their

121
00:10:04,280 --> 00:10:09,757
grandparents, and did not forgive it, and did not
tear it down either, and are still —

122
00:10:09,777 --> 00:10:15,510
four centuries after the last panel went out in an
office on the sixth floor of a building

123
00:10:15,530 --> 00:10:19,282
that was always there — shouting at
each other about trams.

124
00:10:20,231 --> 00:10:22,042
That is the one that kept going.

125
00:10:22,442 --> 00:10:29,083
The narrator would like to record, finally, that
nobody in it needed Albert Wesker to

126
00:10:29,103 --> 00:10:29,631
say so.
